As I promised the second part of our working remotely lecture.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:15,120
And here we are going to talk about online meetings, right.

4
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:19,740
Because now we have so much more of those in our lives.

5
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:26,700
And again, we need some new vocabulary for for them.

6
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:27,180
Right.

7
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:28,680
New words to use.

8
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:31,200
So this part is exactly about it.

9
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:33,720
So it's going to be very practical.

10
00:00:34,950 --> 00:00:35,800
Okay.

11
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:42,150
So the basics, first, those meetings, usually we just say a meeting.

12
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:47,970
This is the most general word, but sometimes you can hear conference call.

13
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:53,310
So conference call is technically a phone call, right?

14
00:00:53,310 --> 00:00:57,090
So there are people in different locations in different places.

15
00:00:57,090 --> 00:01:01,170
They are all talking to each other over the phone.

16
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:02,610
That's conference call.

17
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:06,900
And you can say to be in a call or to have a call.

18
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:12,510
Now, we also have, of course, obviously videoconference.

19
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:17,370
That would be a meeting on Zoom.

20
00:01:17,370 --> 00:01:23,220
Right, or any other software, a video conference.

21
00:01:23,430 --> 00:01:25,620
So this is not a phone.

22
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:29,130
This is not using a phone, but using some app.

23
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:31,560
All right.

24
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:34,410
And here are some sentences.

25
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:36,360
Sorry, I'm in a meeting.

26
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:38,580
I'll get back to you later.

27
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:44,370
So to be in a meeting, as I said, meeting is the most general word for this.

28
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:48,960
I was having a meeting, so I missed a delivery man.

29
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:55,910
So he knocked on my door, but I didn't hear it, I was having a meeting, right.

30
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:03,340
And last one, we are having a conference call with the client where we'll discuss possible solutions

31
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:04,840
to this issue.

32
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:06,980
Okay.

33
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:10,410
Oh, actually, yeah.

34
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:11,400
This one.

35
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:14,220
I was having a meeting, right.

36
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:16,020
I was having a meeting.

37
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:18,300
This is Past Continuous.

38
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:24,090
And we're going to talk about this tense in our grammar section.

39
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:25,590
So wait for it.

40
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,460
I'll right. That next one.

41
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:31,110
Back to back meetings.

42
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:35,010
Meetings without a break in a row.

43
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:41,340
So I finish my previous one at ten and then at ten I start the next one.

44
00:02:43,070 --> 00:02:45,260
All right, here we go.

45
00:02:45,590 --> 00:02:50,060
I had five back to back meetings today, and I'm exhausted.

46
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:52,540
Well, I'm exhausted even reading that.

47
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:53,030
Right.

48
00:02:53,030 --> 00:02:56,900
So back to back to back meetings are really hard.

49
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:07,510
But unfortunately, a lot of us have to do them. The next one. To reschedule, to change time of the meetings.

50
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:14,330
So, for example, it was planned for 11, but we are going to have it at 12. Reschedule.

51
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:17,450
So in general to change time.

52
00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:23,540
But then we can be more specific and we can say to move a meeting up,

53
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:33,260
which means to start earlier or to move a meeting back, which means to start later.

54
00:03:35,490 --> 00:03:37,830
That is quite confusing.

55
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:41,360
Those things like move a meeting up, move the meeting back.

56
00:03:41,370 --> 00:03:44,640
Why do we why do we even say it like move a meeting up?

57
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:45,000
Right.

58
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,520
It sounds weird, but imagine a calendar.

59
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:53,880
It's much easier if you imagine a calendar.

60
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,500
Well, for example, a Google calendar, I'll show you.

61
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:06,070
So for example, we have a meeting planned for 8 p.m.

62
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:11,580
That's definitely too late and we want to start earlier.

63
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:15,950
So we moved it up, right.

64
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:18,000
We moved it up an hour.

65
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:18,950
Right.

66
00:04:19,019 --> 00:04:20,480
An hour and 15 minutes here.

67
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:21,000
Right.

68
00:04:21,779 --> 00:04:27,750
So actually, we are going to start early and move up means to start earlier.

69
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:32,580
Well, to start later is not to move down.

70
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:34,170
That would be logical.

71
00:04:34,290 --> 00:04:37,360
But sometimes language is not language logical.

72
00:04:37,380 --> 00:04:38,580
Sorry about that.

73
00:04:38,730 --> 00:04:42,720
And to start later is move back.

74
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:50,550
So we moved back an hour or 2 hours and now we're going to start at nine.

75
00:04:51,510 --> 00:04:52,240
Perfect.

76
00:04:52,260 --> 00:04:54,450
Now, let's look at some examples.

77
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:58,590
The meeting was rescheduled for Thursday.

78
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,000
So we changed it for Thursday.

79
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:01,920
It was on Monday.

80
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:03,330
We changed it for Thursday.

81
00:05:03,990 --> 00:05:06,360
Sorry, I have my English class at nine.

82
00:05:06,390 --> 00:05:09,360
Let's move our meeting back and hour.

83
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:13,210
Which means we're going to start when?

84
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:16,370
Right at ten.

85
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:18,550
If you said ten.

86
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:19,100
Good.

87
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:23,150
The next one. Tom, the noon meeting was moved up,

88
00:05:23,150 --> 00:05:24,530
so we are starting now.

89
00:05:24,530 --> 00:05:25,520
Will you join?

90
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:30,020
So this is like this wasn't planned.

91
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:31,310
We made the change quickly.

92
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:32,720
We are asking, will you join?

93
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:34,250
Are you able to join?

94
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:37,580
All right.

95
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:40,820
And the next one to share screen.

96
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:42,260
To share screen.

97
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:46,490
Well, everybody knows what it is.

98
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:52,180
I think that hardly any online meeting can go without it.

99
00:05:52,190 --> 00:05:52,790
Right.

100
00:05:52,970 --> 00:05:56,810
So to show your screen to other participants of an online meeting.

101
00:05:57,770 --> 00:06:03,620
But what we want to pay attention to here, we can use it as a verb.

102
00:06:04,490 --> 00:06:09,170
Like, here, let me show you the screen so you can see the chart.

103
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:10,610
Can everybody see it?

104
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:11,750
Can everybody see it?

105
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:12,870
Can everybody see it?

106
00:06:12,890 --> 00:06:13,250
Right.

107
00:06:13,250 --> 00:06:14,630
So it's good to check.

108
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:18,110
And as I said, you're using it as a verb.

109
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:19,670
Share the screen.

110
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:22,110
All right, this is done.

111
00:06:22,190 --> 00:06:22,910
The next one.

112
00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:28,670
Okay, I'll stop sharing my screen now and we can go into detail after the break.

113
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:31,340
I'll stop sharing my screen.

114
00:06:31,790 --> 00:06:33,770
I'll stop sharing my screen now.

115
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:37,130
And we will go into detail after the break.

116
00:06:37,310 --> 00:06:42,230
So another useful phrase here, go into detail.

117
00:06:42,770 --> 00:06:45,210
Well, to pay more attention to it.

118
00:06:45,230 --> 00:06:45,830
Right.

119
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:51,600
Okay, this is done and we move on to the next one.

120
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:55,080
Useful phrases for online meetings.

121
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:59,370
So what to say during those meetings?

122
00:06:59,370 --> 00:07:00,180
Right.

123
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:06,180
So when you don't hear someone, for example, or you don't understand something, what to say in those

124
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:07,170
situations?

125
00:07:07,770 --> 00:07:09,720
Here is a handful of

126
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:12,080
useful phrases for you.

127
00:07:13,580 --> 00:07:18,890
So during those meetings, that can be a mess, right.

128
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:21,410
Some people may be on audio, some

129
00:07:21,890 --> 00:07:22,270
so

130
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:24,500
so you can see, can't see their faces.

131
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:30,310
And sometimes it's a good idea to introduce yourself.

132
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:30,970
Right.

133
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:34,850
So you can say Mike's speaking, right.

134
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:36,110
Lilly speaking.

135
00:07:36,110 --> 00:07:39,620
I would say Lilly speaking or Lilly here.

136
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:40,010
Right.

137
00:07:40,010 --> 00:07:40,640
Lilly here.

138
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:42,500
I just wanted to ask something.

139
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:50,540
So it's especially important to say this when you are just on audio, so your camera is off so people

140
00:07:50,540 --> 00:07:52,730
know who is speaking.

141
00:07:53,330 --> 00:07:58,580
Well, again, if you didn't say it, someone may ask, sorry, who's speaking?

142
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:03,500
So again, it's okay to check and this is how you can do it.

143
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:05,120
Sorry, who's speaking?

144
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:07,300
And then you answer.

145
00:08:07,310 --> 00:08:10,130
This is Anna, for example, or this is Lilly.

146
00:08:12,570 --> 00:08:14,640
Next one. Interruptions.

147
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:19,250
Sometimes we have to interrupt and to ask a question quickly before we forgot.

148
00:08:19,260 --> 00:08:19,710
Right.

149
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:21,990
So what can we say?

150
00:08:24,060 --> 00:08:26,960
It's a good idea to start with

151
00:08:26,970 --> 00:08:27,700
sorry.

152
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:28,380
Of course,

153
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:28,920
right.

154
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:30,570
And you can say, Sorry,

155
00:08:30,630 --> 00:08:32,669
could I jump in for a second?

156
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:35,490
Could I jump in for a second?

157
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:38,020
So to interrupt someone quickly.

158
00:08:39,159 --> 00:08:39,940
The next one.

159
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:41,150
Let me clarify.

160
00:08:41,169 --> 00:08:43,299
What is the deadline for this task?

161
00:08:43,929 --> 00:08:46,450
So someone is talking, talking, talking.

162
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:50,360
But you want to clarify something, right.

163
00:08:50,380 --> 00:08:53,470
You want to make sure, if you understood correctly.

164
00:08:53,710 --> 00:08:55,700
So you would say, let me clarify,

165
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:57,850
what is the deadline for this task?

166
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:00,130
All right.

167
00:09:00,140 --> 00:09:01,160
And the last one.

168
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:02,880
I didn't get it.

169
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:04,580
Could you repeat the last bit, please?

170
00:09:04,730 --> 00:09:06,260
Sorry, I didn't get it.

171
00:09:06,350 --> 00:09:08,390
Could you repeat the last bit, please?

172
00:09:10,070 --> 00:09:18,050
So when you didn't hear someone, right, and before the conversation goes on and on and on, you may

173
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:22,490
ask, could you repeat the last bit, the last part?

174
00:09:24,730 --> 00:09:25,870
All right.

175
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:30,280
The next one. Comment while you're doing something.

176
00:09:30,580 --> 00:09:31,630
Okay.

177
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:33,580
Awkward silences, right.

178
00:09:33,580 --> 00:09:39,160
So sometimes you have to do something to find something to open something and

179
00:09:41,110 --> 00:09:43,840
you feel the urge to say something.

180
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:47,600
Well, you can comment on what you are doing.

181
00:09:47,630 --> 00:09:51,920
Like here, I'm going to switch over to my

182
00:09:51,920 --> 00:09:52,850
Presentation.

183
00:09:52,850 --> 00:09:53,190
Right.

184
00:09:53,210 --> 00:09:58,070
So you are actually saying what you're going to do next.

185
00:09:58,340 --> 00:10:01,040
I'm going to switch over to my presentation.

186
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:06,290
So for this, you used to be going to. The next one.

187
00:10:06,470 --> 00:10:07,340
Hold on.

188
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:09,380
I'm trying to share my screen.

189
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:11,960
So hold on.

190
00:10:12,410 --> 00:10:14,510
Means wait a second.

191
00:10:14,510 --> 00:10:14,830
Right.

192
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:16,010
A good phrase to use.

193
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:17,030
Hold on.

194
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:18,960
Wait a second, please.

195
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:25,730
I'm trying to share my screen, so I'm trying to do something where you can say.

196
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:28,690
Hold on, I'm opening the document.

197
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:29,110
Right.

198
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:38,840
So you use present continuous here, right, to comment on things you are doing right now. And Present

199
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:39,650
Continuous 

200
00:10:39,860 --> 00:10:43,730
we actually have this covered in our previous course.

201
00:10:43,730 --> 00:10:44,090
Right.

202
00:10:44,090 --> 00:10:46,520
So you can check it out.

203
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:49,070
The next one.

204
00:10:49,070 --> 00:10:52,190
What can ruin an online meeting?

205
00:10:52,610 --> 00:10:55,580
Obviously technical problems, right

206
00:10:57,220 --> 00:11:00,610
What kind of technical problems may we have?

207
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:03,340
Mute unmute.

208
00:11:03,880 --> 00:11:09,910
So to have your microphone off or on. Freeze.

209
00:11:09,910 --> 00:11:13,690
So this happens when someone doesn't move.

210
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:14,640
Right.

211
00:11:14,740 --> 00:11:17,400
So there is some problem with the Internet.

212
00:11:17,410 --> 00:11:20,230
Maybe the entrance is down for the second or

213
00:11:20,830 --> 00:11:23,250
just slow,

214
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:23,860
right.

215
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:26,150
Then a person freezes.

216
00:11:26,170 --> 00:11:28,810
Well, on the next slide we will use it in a sentence.

217
00:11:29,020 --> 00:11:31,120
You'll see it. And a delay.

218
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:40,540
So when you see and hear things a few seconds later. Again, due to slow internet connection, we may

219
00:11:40,750 --> 00:11:41,350
see and hear 

220
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:43,050
things a few seconds later.

221
00:11:44,510 --> 00:11:45,590
So what to say?

222
